WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:04.170
Comparison is the next type of operator to
cover.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:09.190
For this example, I have three player scores
on the first three lines of this 142-comparison

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:10.389
file.

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:17.100
On this first observation, players 1 and 3
have the same score, and player 2 has a higher

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.100
score.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.490
Lines 5 through 7 contain print statements
with comparisons.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:27.580
The double equal sign on line 5 is a very
important point to notice.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:32.570
Python and other programs use a double equal
sign as a comparison to make it distinct from

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:36.160
a single equal sign, which is an assignment.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.890
To further impress this point, watch what
happens when I change this operator to a single

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:45.320
equal sign and run the code.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.160
We get an error message because you can’t
assign a variable a value in the middle of

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.620
a print statement.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:56.570
I will return this to a double-equal sign
and rerun the code.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:02.079
The code now runs without error, and the first
statement is False, as the two player scores

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:04.930
being evaluated are not equal.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.430
Speaking of not equal.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:12.909
Python uses an exclamation point and an equal
sign or what some refer to as a bang and equal

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:16.020
sign to signify not equal to.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:23.070
I will change the first equal sign to a bang
and rerun the code.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.080
The statement is now true.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.600
Let’s evaluate the second and third statements.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.960
Both statements return a value of false.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.880
Let’s see why.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:43.619
Player 1 and Player 3 have identical scores,
so Player 1’s score is not lower.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:51.710
The third statement is false because Player
1’s score is lower, not higher, than player

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.010
2’s score.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:57.430
Since we are covering comparisons, we want
to make sure we understand the difference

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:02.030
between less than and less than or equal to
and greater than and greater than or equal

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.740
to.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.710
Comparisons in programming work just like
comparisons in math.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:13.379
As a case in point, I will change the comparison
on line 6 from less than to less than or equal

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:16.579
to and rerun the code.

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:24.160
The second statement is now true, as player
1 and 3 have the same score.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.769
This change reminds me of a fundamental principle
in programming: fully understanding what someone

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:31.519
wants before writing code.

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:37.270
Often, people may say, “Well, I want to
know if this is lower than that.”

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:43.200
But, do they mean lower, or what they want
could be the same or lower?

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.150
That is the difference between something like
less than, or less than or equal to, as we

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.150
saw here.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:55.400
In other words, we need to know the true logic
someone wants for an app to code the app properly

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.659
and have the app generate the proper results,
as demonstrated in this example.

